    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Value of an Unknown Constant Given a PDF",
    "problem": "Solve for $k$ given that the following function is a probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k,\\, & 0<x<2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nFor the given function, the second condition states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nComputing the integral on the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+k)\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{x}^{3}+kx]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{8}{12}+2k]-[0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}+2k=1\\,⟹\\,k=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$",
